\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



Represent a placement of $n$ queens by a permutation $\pi$ of $[n]$: the queens occupy $(i,\pi(i))$. Nonattack additionally requires the numbers $i-\pi(i)$ to be distinct and the numbers $i+\pi(i)$ to be distinct. Let $Q(n)$ count such permutations on the labelled board, without identifying rotations or reflections. The target is an asymptotic formula for this count. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2023.109127}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
In how many ways (asymptotically) $Q(n)$ may $n$ non-attacking queens be placed on an $n \times n$ chessboard? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Scope and interpretation.}

The archive reports the leading logarithmic asymptotic as established. The residual question is an asymptotic enumeration beyond that precision; the known exponential-scale estimate is not being relabelled as open. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Obtain an asymptotic formula with multiplicative relative error, or otherwise determine the subexponential factor beyond Simkin's exponential rate. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
How many ways can n mutually nonattacking queens be placed on a labelled n by n board, to asymptotically precise accuracy?

